[{:seon.task/name "one-monotonic-clock"
  :seon.task/plan "Session DUP-HELPERS, audit row A6. What exists: three monotonic clocks, mixed even inside one function (seon.writer stamps with sock's and times with lib's; seon.root likewise), and 60-odd raw System/nanoTime reads beside them. seon.lib/now-ns is System/nanoTime (Jolt's: 102 ns and 225 bytes a read through its contract, 1 us resolution); seon.profile/now-ns is Chez's (current-time 'time-monotonic): 28 ns, 48 bytes, 1 us resolution, and counted from this process's start, so two processes' readings cannot be compared; seon.sock/now-ns is clock_gettime_nsec_np(CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW), the clock every process shares, but through lib/defc's contract wrapper: 75-97 ns and 531 bytes a read. Measured 2026-10-22 in a REPL on the M1: the same CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW read as a Chez foreign-procedure called from Scheme costs 9.5 ns, allocates nothing and resolves 41 ns; a forked child reads the same timeline. The one clock: seon.profile's Scheme seon-profile-now becomes that foreign read, and seon.profile/now-ns (uncontracted, as the meter is: seon.profile is not metered) is the one reader every namespace uses; seon.profile/ms-since (moved from seon.lib, which seon.contract and seon.db cannot require) is the one 'ms since t0'. seon.lib/now-ns and seon.sock/now-ns are deleted, and System/nanoTime in src and test is replaced by them. The profiler gains a finer clock (its samples were quantized to 1 us). What v1 did: System/nanoTime on the JVM (one clock, shared within one process). The field's way: one monotonic clock source per program (CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW / mach_absolute_time on Darwin). Wiki: docs/machine.md#the-clock names the owner."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.profile-test/the-clock-is-the-one-every-process-reads seon.profile-test/reading-the-clock-allocates-nothing seon.profile-test/every-touched-function-has-an-estimated-time}}]
